<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:ce825ba1c088d7cf823bcee082a00943ff5f7795> <http://schema.org/additionalProperty> _:cb144be9fdde1ba8ffdaeae816d655b3 .
_:cb144be9fdde1ba8ffdaeae816d655b3 <http://schema.org/value> "FEPA" .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:ce825ba1c088d7cf823bcee082a00943ff5f7795> <http://schema.org/description> "As part of the monitoring programme stipulated within the FEPA Licence for the construction of a 129MW offshore windfarm on the Kentish Flats (Thames Estuary), fish monitoring surveys are required as little is known about the potential effects of wind farms in terms of enhancing or aggregating fish populations. Advice detailed in Annex I of the FEPA Licence (Licence 31780/03/0) for the Kentish Flats Wind Farm states that ‘adequate pre-construction baseline and post-construction surveys of fish populations in the area of the wind farm’ need to be undertaken.\r\n\r\nThree pre-construction surveys were undertaken. The first of the seasonal post-construction fish monitoring surveys was undertaken on 7th July 2005 (see separate report). The second post-construction survey, was conducted on the 12th October 2005.\r\n\r\nSubsequent surveys were planned for December/January 2005 and March/April 2006, to encompass the projected first operational year of the wind-farm. The survey planned for December 2005 or January 2006 was unable to take place due to constant problems with the weather. The survey was subsequently delayed by engine problems encountered by the fishing vessel used, ‘Our Sarah Jane’. The current survey was undertaken over one day, 23rd March 2006. The final post construction survey is planned for June and will reflect the pre-construction survey activity.\r\n\r\nDetails of catch composition and subsequent analysis are not included here, as these data will be compiled into an interpretative report following completion of all the field surveys." .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:ce825ba1c088d7cf823bcee082a00943ff5f7795> <http://schema.org/additionalProperty> _:a42eae44ed2734b323ed053df3f71f42 .
