_:d74e90ba1d244704b5e7752fcab13f71 <http://schema.org/value> "Greater Wash SEA area" .
_:f0912f6d04c1dfede43bc7096aa15167 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/PropertyValue> .
_:d74e90ba1d244704b5e7752fcab13f71 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/PropertyValue> .
_:ca6af902393b660f53ffcfdea5734a78 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/PropertyValue> .
_:f0912f6d04c1dfede43bc7096aa15167 <http://schema.org/value> "assessment\r\ntools" .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:89fc352853e2a72407360043a1e8e75821d315b1> <http://schema.org/description> "The potential impact of the development of offshore wind farms (OWFs) upon seabird\r\npopulations is a key issue within the current debate on sustainable energy provision. Terns,\r\n(Sterna spp. and Sternula albifrons), especially when on their breeding grounds, have emerged\r\nas sensitive receptors in many consented and planned OWF projects. To date, information on\r\nthe interaction of terns with OWFs has remained scant due to a lack of understanding of the\r\nforaging ecology of terns especially at specific sites and the inability of standard assessment\r\ntools to define the relative use of specific areas by foraging terns.\r\nThe current project aimed to advance the use of a series of methodological and assessment\r\ntools, some of which were initially developed in the Greater Wash SEA area (funded by\r\nCentrica/AMEC) with others developed specifically in this project. In essence, this involved a\r\nmove towards an individual-based approach." .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:89fc352853e2a72407360043a1e8e75821d315b1> <http://schema.org/additionalProperty> _:b98b5460f2a9ef346d2ce1f33b799e84 .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:89fc352853e2a72407360043a1e8e75821d315b1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/Dataset> .
_:f3696ce8f0c8b04260b2bc8979952aac <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/PropertyValue> .
_:dadff9e32be7f34b65c05f47791ae001 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/PropertyValue> .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:89f644a79d1f35aa97dd73f2b570576518288996> <http://schema.org/additionalProperty> _:dadff9e32be7f34b65c05f47791ae001 .
_:dadff9e32be7f34b65c05f47791ae001 <http://schema.org/value> "baseline monitoring" .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:89f644a79d1f35aa97dd73f2b570576518288996> <http://schema.org/description> "Site Condition Monitoring (SCM) was inaugurated in the Loch Laxford SAC in 2009. In 2015, a subset of survey sites were selected to represent good geographical coverage of the SAC from the baseline monitoring sites that were established and examined in 2009. The aim of this study was to (1) identify any deterioration in the condition of the protected marine features 'large shallow inlets and bays' and 'reefs', and (2) to form a judgement on their current condition. This work consisted of a drop down video drift survey at 70 sites and a detailed reef and mixed reef/sediment habitat survey along 3 relocatable transects. A maerl bed dive transect survey was carried out at the same 3 maerl bed sites examined in the baseline survey. This survey was opportunistic, representing a contingency plan to utilize poor weather conditions on a scheduled cruise, hence the reduced scope of the survey to examine a subset of the baseline monitoring sites established in 2009. Survey work was carried out from the vessel MV Halton." .
_:c275b343deaf29d40eea9a5ebacb04f9 <http://schema.org/value> "maerl bed dive transect survey" .
_:eebb0060b707791d30b50b27efc0290a <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/PropertyValue> .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:89f644a79d1f35aa97dd73f2b570576518288996> <http://schema.org/additionalProperty> _:eebb0060b707791d30b50b27efc0290a .
