<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:30159f521e560033340b1c33029fb98113c5d521> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/Dataset> .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:30159f521e560033340b1c33029fb98113c5d521> <http://schema.org/additionalProperty> _:a4e98b3b62d1c54778677f48b45975d0 .
_:a8214e9d5104ce6593e25c0bde2f9ad8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/PropertyValue> .
_:d114b3024f3a7aa8ebacf3a64f99e8f <http://schema.org/value> "beam trawl survey" .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:30159f521e560033340b1c33029fb98113c5d521> <http://schema.org/description> "This project examines the beam trawl survey that took place across the proposed development area of Rhyl Flats offshore wind farm during September 2006 by CMACS Ltd. This survey was carried ou to provide information concerning the benthic populations as part of the benthic baseline survey program. In total 954 fish from 23 fish taxa were recorded. The most common fish species recorded was dab, with a total of 229 individuals recorded. In comparison with the beam trawl results from 2005, fish species and epifaunal numbers are similar to those recorded at the same sites. It is considered that these two datasets from successive years (2005 and 2006) will provide significant information from which to monitor any possible impacts of the Rhyl Flats offshore wind farm construction and operation upon the benthic fish and epifaunal species populations of the area." .
_:a4e98b3b62d1c54778677f48b45975d0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/PropertyValue> .
_:a4e98b3b62d1c54778677f48b45975d0 <http://schema.org/name> "organizations" .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:30159f521e560033340b1c33029fb98113c5d521> <http://schema.org/additionalProperty> _:a8214e9d5104ce6593e25c0bde2f9ad8 .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:30159f521e560033340b1c33029fb98113c5d521> <http://schema.org/additionalProperty> _:d114b3024f3a7aa8ebacf3a64f99e8f .
_:e9411215bb8aa367ee3836b99fb53635 <http://schema.org/value> "English waters" .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:300ce26ec38a67695db185c5005e65e0f320f821> <http://schema.org/additionalProperty> _:e9411215bb8aa367ee3836b99fb53635 .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:300ce26ec38a67695db185c5005e65e0f320f821> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/Dataset> .
_:e9411215bb8aa367ee3836b99fb53635 <http://schema.org/value> "Bristol Channel" .
<urn:gleaner.io:oih:medin:data:300ce26ec38a67695db185c5005e65e0f320f821> <http://schema.org/description> "This project was commissioned to consider if we might be able to move from generic to more specific understandings of potential with regard to submerged prehistory, and how these approaches might be evaluated. In this light a study area was chosen that would allow leverage of onshore and offshore data gained over several decades of research; the Bristol Channel, Somerset Levels and Severn Estuary region. In particular this project had three overarching goals:\r\n Assess the efficacy of predictive modelling for the determination of the potential for submerged prehistoric archaeology in English waters;\r\n Evaluate the range of methods open to archaeologists when assessing the potential for submerged prehistoric archaeology offshore, and how best to investigate/mitigate for it; and\r\n Extend our knowledge of key inter-tidal and offshore sequences in a region already known for its nationally significant inter-tidal and onshore prehistoric record." .
_:e9411215bb8aa367ee3836b99fb53635 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/PropertyValue> .
_:e9411215bb8aa367ee3836b99fb53635 <http://schema.org/value> "Severn Estuary region" .
_:e9411215bb8aa367ee3836b99fb53635 <http://schema.org/name> "geospatial region" .
_:e9411215bb8aa367ee3836b99fb53635 <http://schema.org/value> "Somerset Levels" .
